### Step 3: Implement Smart Batching

Smart batching involves grouping documents into batches that are processed together. This can help balance the load and optimize resource usage.

#### Example: Smart Batching

```java
public class BatchIngestionService {
    private static final int MAX_BATCH_SIZE = 100; // Adjust based on your requirements

    public void ingestDocuments(List<Document> documents) {
        List<List<Document>> batches = createBatches(documents, MAX_BATCH_SIZE);
        for (List<Document> batch : batches) {
            processBatch(batch);
        }
    }

    private List<List<Document>> createBatches(List<Document> documents, int batchSize) {
        List<List<Document>> batches = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < documents.size(); i += batchSize) {
            int end = Math.min(i + batchSize, documents.size());
            batches.add(documents.subList(i, end));
        }
        return batches;
    }

    private void processBatch(List<Document> batch) {
        // Process the batch of documents
        for (Document document : batch) {
            // Handle each document
            System.out.println("Processing document: " + document.getId());
        }
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Use Efficient Storage and Retrieval Mechanisms

For large files, consider using efficient storage mechanisms like chunked storage or distributed file systems. Also, use caching mechanisms to avoid reprocessing the same data multiple times.

#### Example: Chunked Storage

```java
public class ChunkedStorage {
    private static final int CHUNK_SIZE = 1024 * 1024; // 1 MB chunks

    public void storeDocumentInChunks(Document document, String storagePath) throws IOException {
        byte[] content = document.getContent().getBytes();
        int numChunks = (content.length + CHUNK_SIZE - 1) / CHUNK_SIZE;

        for (int i = 0; i < numChunks; i++) {
            int start = i * CHUNK_SIZE;
            int end = Math.min(start + CHUNK_SIZE, content.length);
            byte[] chunk = Arrays.copyOfRange(content, start, end);
            String chunkFilePath = storagePath + "/chunk_" + i;
            Files.write(Paths.get(chunkFilePath), chunk);
        }
    }
}
```

