Integrating error tracking for file parsing failures in a custom NiFi processor is essential for maintaining the reliability and robustness of your data ingestion pipeline. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you implement error tracking in your custom NiFi processor:

### Step 1: Define Error Handling Mechanism

First, define how you want to handle and track errors. You can log errors, send notifications, or store error details in a database.

### Step 2: Modify the Custom Processor

Modify your custom processor to include error handling and tracking mechanisms. This involves catching exceptions during file parsing and logging or storing the error details.

#### Example Custom NiFi Processor with Error Tracking

```java
import org.apache.nifi.annotation.behavior.EventDriven;
import org.apache.nifi.annotation.documentation.CapabilityDescription;
import org.apache.nifi.annotation.documentation.Tags;
import org.apache.nifi.components.PropertyDescriptor;
import org.apache.nifi.flowfile.FlowFile;
import org.apache.nifi.processor.AbstractProcessor;
import org.apache.nifi.processor.ProcessContext;
import org.apache.nifi.processor.ProcessSession;
import org.apache.nifi.processor.Relationship;
import org.apache.nifi.processor.util.StandardValidators;

import java.io.InputStream;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

@EventDriven
@Tags({"ingestion", "document"})
@CapabilityDescription("Processes documents using a modular ingestion service.")
public class DocumentIngestionProcessor extends AbstractProcessor {

    public static final Relationship REL_SUCCESS = new Relationship.Builder()
            .name("success")
            .description("Success relationship")
            .build();

    public static final Relationship REL_FAILURE = new Relationship.Builder()
            .name("failure")
            .description("Failure relationship")
            .build();

    private List<PropertyDescriptor> properties;
    private Set<Relationship> relationships;

    private BatchIngestionService ingestionService;

    @Override
    protected void init(final Properties props) {
        final List<PropertyDescriptor> properties = Collections.emptyList();
        final Set<Relationship> relationships = Collections.emptySet();
        this.properties = properties;
        this.relationships = relationships;

        ingestionService = new BatchIngestionService();
    }

    @Override
    public Set<Relationship> getRelationships() {
        return Collections.singleton(REL_SUCCESS);
    }

    @Override
    public final List<PropertyDescriptor> getSupportedPropertyDescriptors() {
        return properties;
    }

    @Override
    public void onTrigger(final ProcessContext context, final ProcessSession session) {
        List<FlowFile> flowFiles = session.get(10); // Adjust batch size as needed
        if (flowFiles.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Document> documents = new ArrayList<>();
        for (FlowFile flowFile : flowFiles) {
            try (InputStream inputStream = session.read(flowFile)) {
                String content = new String(inputStream.readAllBytes(), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
                String format = flowFile.getAttribute("document.format");
                documents.add(new Document(flowFile.getAttribute("document.id"), content, format));
            } catch (IOException e) {
                getLogger().error("Error reading flow file {}: {}", flowFile, e);
                session.transfer(flowFile, REL_FAILURE);
            }
        }

        try {
            ingestionService.ingestDocuments(documents);
            for (FlowFile flowFile : flowFiles) {
                session.transfer(flowFile, REL_SUCCESS);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            getLogger().error("Error processing documents: {}", e);
            for (FlowFile flowFile : flowFiles) {
                session.transfer(flowFile, REL_FAILURE);
            }
        }
    }

    private class BatchIngestionService {
        private static final int MAX_BATCH_SIZE = 100; // Adjust based on your requirements

        public void ingestDocuments(List<Document> documents) {
            List<List<Document>> batches = createBatches(documents, MAX_BATCH_SIZE);
            for (List<Document> batch : batches) {
                processBatch(batch);
            }
        }

        private List<List<Document>> createBatches(List<Document> documents, int batchSize) {
            List<List<Document>> batches = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = _0; i < documents.size(); i += batchSize) {
                int end = Math.min(i + batchSize, documents.size());
                batches.add(documents.subList(i, end));
            }
            return batches;
        }

        private void processBatch(List<Document> batch) {
            for (Document document : batch) {
                try {
                    handleDocument(document);
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    getLogger().error("Error handling document {}: {}", document.getId(), e);
                    // Optionally, you can add the error to a list or database for further analysis
                }
            }
        }

        private void handleDocument(Document document) {
            // Your document handling logic here
            System.out.println("Handling document: " + document.getId());
        }
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Logging Errors

In the example above, errors are logged using `getLogger().error`. You can customize this to include more detailed information such as the document ID, file path, and specific error messages.

### Step 4: Storing Errors

If you want to store errors for further analysis, you can use a database or a file system to persist error details.

#### Example: Storing Errors in a Database

