Implementing rate limiting in your Spring Security setup can help prevent abuse and protect your API from excessive requests. There are several ways to implement rate limiting, but one of the most common and effective approaches is to use a library like Spring Cloud Gateway or a standalone rate-limiting solution like Resilience4j.

### Using Spring Cloud Gateway for Rate Limiting

Spring Cloud Gateway is a powerful tool for managing API gateways and can easily integrate rate limiting into your application. Here's how you can set it up:

#### 1. Add Dependencies

Add the necessary dependencies to your `pom.xml` or `build.gradle` file.

For Maven:
```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-gateway</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

For Gradle:
```groovy
implementation 'org.springframework.cloud:spring-cloud-starter-gateway'
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-actuator'
```

#### 2. Configure Rate Limiting

Create a configuration class to set up rate limiting in Spring Cloud Gateway.

```java
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.cors.CorsConfiguration;

@Configuration
public class GatewayConfig {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator gatewayRoutes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
            .route(r -> r.path("/api/**")
                .filters(f -> f.requestRateLimiter(config -> config.setRateLimiter(redisRateLimiter())))
                .uri("lb://your-service"))
            .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public RequestRateLimiter redisRateLimiter() {
        return new RedisRateLimiter(redisConnectionFactory(), 10, Duration.ofSeconds(1));
    }

    @Bean
    public ConnectionFactory redisConnectionFactory() {
        LettuceConnectionFactory factory = new LettuceConnectionFactory();
        factory.setHostName("localhost");
        factory.setPort(6379);
        return factory;
    }
}
```

#### 3. Configure Redis

Ensure you have Redis running and configured properly. The `RedisRateLimiter` uses Redis to store rate-limiting state.

#### 4. Application Properties

Configure your application properties to enable Spring Cloud Gateway and Redis.

```properties
spring.cloud.gateway.routes[0].id=api_route
spring.cloud.gateway.routes[0].uri=lb://your-service
spring.cloud.gateway.routes[0].predicates[0]=Path=/api/**
spring.cloud.gateway.routes[0].filters[0]=RequestRateLimiter

spring.redis.host=localhost
spring.redis.port=6379
```

### Using Resilience4j for Rate Limiting

Resilience4j is another popular library for implementing rate limiting and circuit breaking.

#### 1. Add Dependencies

Add the necessary dependencies to your `pom.xml` or `build.gradle` file.

For Maven:
```xml
